WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:17.000
Did you know that some birds have a built-in&nbsp;
GPS that's so precise, they can always return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:22.440
to the exact same nesting spot, even after being&nbsp;
away for years and having travelled thousands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:29.040
miles across vast oceans and continents? It's&nbsp;
a skill we humans would envy. I once considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:34.760
calling mountain rescue after searching for&nbsp;
my car in a multi-storey for over 30 minutes.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:39.000
It’s thought that birds exploit the&nbsp;
Earth's magnetic field, star patterns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.760
and even olfactory cues in the air to&nbsp;
achieve such remarkable navigational feats.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.240
Take the Bar-tailed Godwit, which holds the&nbsp;
record for the longest nonstop flight by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.240 --> 00:01:00.160
bird. A female specimen was recorded flying 8,435&nbsp;
miles nonstop from Alaska to Tasmania. The journey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:06.360
took about nine days, without landing once for&nbsp;
rest or food. Furthermore, it did this across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:14.120
completely open ocean, without any prominent&nbsp;
landmarks to guide its way, not even a Starbucks.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:18.000
Then, there’s the Arctic Tern - known&nbsp;
for having the longest migration of any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.840
animal. This small bird travels from the&nbsp;
Arctic to the Antarctic and back again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:29.840
every year – that's a journey spanning close to&nbsp;
25,000 miles - and somehow they never get lost.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:35.040
Over its 30-year lifespan your average arctic&nbsp;
tern will fly enough distance to equal going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:41.080
to the moon and back. Yet, despite this&nbsp;
remarkable capability the Arctic Tern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:52.800
isn’t the GOAT of the avian world. There’s a&nbsp;
much, much larger bird that is the undisputed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:59.560
master of the sky. To see one in flight is&nbsp;
to witness one of the miracles of nature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:05.440
And everything about it pushes the boundaries&nbsp;
of what physics tells us should be possible.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:11.280
This winged enigma is found roaming the vast&nbsp;
arctic ocean. It’s the largest flying bird&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:16.360
on earth, with a wingspan that stretches well&nbsp;
over 3 and a half metres - that’s bigger than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:23.480
my car… and my house, which is just this black&nbsp;
box I’m trapped in. I don’t even have a toilet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:28.080
I’ve spent the past 10 years going&nbsp;
in the garden centre across the road.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:33.200
Anyway, this bird can fly&nbsp;
further than most others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.960
yet it rarely beats its wings.&nbsp;
It can live for over 60 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:47.560
yet it only breeds once every two years. Welcome&nbsp;
to the wonderful world of the Wandering Albatross.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:53.240
Despite its awesome size the Albatross is also&nbsp;
known for its ability to travel spectacularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:58.400
far. Often flying over 600 miles a day,&nbsp;
in search of food. And a single Wandering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:04.280
Albatross is expected to cover between&nbsp;
5 to 7 million miles in its lifetime. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:08.040
put that into perspective, your average&nbsp;
human will travel about half a million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:13.800
miles in his lifetime. And that includes&nbsp;
travel in cars, trains and aeroplanes.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:19.960
When one of these majestic seabirds takes to the&nbsp;
air, it’s truly a sight to behold. Its huge wings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:25.560
fan out, as it glides with effortless grace,&nbsp;
rarely needing to flap. Like an astronaut on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:30.200
a spacewalk - it appears to defy gravity.&nbsp;
And it does something that very few other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:38.920
birds can do - it flies for days, even weeks&nbsp;
on end, without ever once landing. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:44.520
an Albatross can travel for years&nbsp;
before returning to solid ground.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:55.680
It’s no wonder these incredible creatures have&nbsp;
been revered by mariners throughout time. During&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.120
the Age of Sail it was believed that the&nbsp;
Wandering Albatross carried the souls of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:05.160
dead sailors. Mostly because they are&nbsp;
known to stalk ships for days on end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:11.000
gliding effortlessly in the ship’s wake. It’s a&nbsp;
haunting sight that has given rise to the saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:17.680
‘to have an albatross around your neck’ which&nbsp;
means one is carrying a large burden or is cursed.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:21.920
But how is it that these birds can stay aloft&nbsp;
for so long? Especially when you consider an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:26.160
adult weighs up to 13kg. The fact that it&nbsp;
can get off the ground in the first place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:31.440
is miraculous, never mind staying up there&nbsp;
for weeks before coming down again. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:37.280
it has a few devilishly clever adaptations&nbsp;
that allow it to do the seemingly impossible.

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:42.840
Firstly, its mighty wingspan of up to 3.5 metres&nbsp;
is one of its greatest assets. You probably hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:47.440
that and think, yeah, it’s basically a big&nbsp;
seagull. But allow me to try and visualise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:53.720
how enormous these things really are. Just&nbsp;
one of its wings is as long as I am tall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:58.920
and both of its wings are the same as two&nbsp;
fully-grown men, standing on top of each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:02.960
other. Which is highly relatable of course,&nbsp;
because we see that all the time when we go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:08.600
outside. And it’s not just its wings that are&nbsp;
grand - the Albatrosses' hefty body is larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:14.360
than a fully-grown turkey. You’re going to&nbsp;
need a bigger oven to cook this bad boy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:21.960
This is a man holding a pretty big turkey.&nbsp;
And this is a man holding an Albatross.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:26.160
I really shouldn’t have to say this, but if&nbsp;
you do happen to chance upon an albatross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.680
please don’t try to cook it - they’re&nbsp;
heavily protected - like “you’ll go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:34.560
to prison for life and get spanked&nbsp;
by David Attenborough” protected.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:39.720
A good way of determining how well a bird can&nbsp;
fly is by measuring its wing loading - this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:45.520
basically the ratio of a bird’s weight to&nbsp;
its wingspan. Birds with low wing-loading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:50.240
are generally better flyers because they’re&nbsp;
able to generate more lift. The Wandering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:55.320
Albatross has a very low wing-loading -&nbsp;
in fact, it’s one of the lowest on earth.

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:59.800
Those impressive wings are also perfectly&nbsp;
formed, with a long, slender profile - in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:03.840
they’re uncannily similar to those&nbsp;
of an aeroplane. When in flight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:08.440
air moves faster over the top of its wings&nbsp;
than it does underneath, creating an area of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:13.400
low pressure on top of the wings, which in turn&nbsp;
creates lift that keeps the bird in the air.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:17.800
Albatrosses also have a tendon-locking&nbsp;
mechanism that lets them "lock" their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:22.640
wings in an extended position,&nbsp;
reducing muscular exertion.

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:28.000
Put all this together and the Wandering&nbsp;
Albatross is able to take off and stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:34.360
in flight in conditions so mild that&nbsp;
most other birds would be grounded.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:39.200
The Albatross is also a true pioneer,&nbsp;
when it comes to conserving energy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:46.720
They do this through a technique called&nbsp;
“Dynamic Soaring''. This bird has special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:50.920
sensory organs within its nostrils, which are&nbsp;
able to detect subtle differences in the wind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:56.880
speed. Basically it can smell the wind.&nbsp;
It uses this power to constantly ride the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:02.600
boundary between faster winds up high&nbsp;
and slower moving air near the water.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:08.360
Turning into the faster air, the Albatross is&nbsp;
lifted higher. Then it plummets downwards at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:15.120
a staggering 70 mph. As it sweeps back into&nbsp;
the slower-moving air, scarily close to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:20.400
water’s surface, where the contrast in wind&nbsp;
speed arrests its downward momentum and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:25.840
“slingshots” it back up into the faster&nbsp;
air, once again. This cycle is repeated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:31.960
continuously allowing it to optimally harness&nbsp;
the wind’s energy. Using dynamic soaring the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:38.840
wandering albatross can travel vast distances&nbsp;
without ever flapping its wings. In doing so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:44.400
it exploits the energy within its&nbsp;
environment, and expends zero of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:52.960
its own. It's a ballet of aerodynamics, with&nbsp;
the vast expanse of the ocean as its stage.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:57.000
But what about water? Surely it needs to&nbsp;
drink whilst soaring for days on end above&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:02.360
the salty ocean? Well, the albatross' bill&nbsp;
houses specialised salt glands. These work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:08.280
tirelessly to extract salt from the seawater&nbsp;
they ingest, producing vital freshwater the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:14.200
bird relies on for hydration. And all that&nbsp;
excess salt is efficiently excreted through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:19.880
their nostrils. If salt started coming out of&nbsp;
my nostrils I’d be hospitalised and my head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:25.720
would be x-rayed - but apparently if you’re&nbsp;
a bird it’s more of a feature than a fault.

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:30.240
But what’s really remarkable about the Wandering&nbsp;
Albatross is that it doesn’t just fly for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:39.400
long time - it lives for a long time too. How&nbsp;
long? Well, in 1956 a mature Laysan albatross&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:45.520
was ringed to keep a track of it. She was named&nbsp;
Wisdom - and she must have been one smart cookie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:53.520
because she was found again in 2021, hatching&nbsp;
yet another chick - making her at least 70 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.520 --> 00:09:02.600
old. That makes Wisdom the oldest confirmed wild&nbsp;
bird in the world. To put that into perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:10.920
a sparrow lives for about 3 years, a bald&nbsp;
eagle lives for about 25. But Albatross&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:16.360
frequently survive over 60 years out in the&nbsp;
wild, and the wild is a bloody terrifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:23.440
place! Some say the albatross could live up&nbsp;
to 100 years, but that’s yet to be verified.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:27.240
Looking at the Wandering Albatross it’s&nbsp;
clear that it’s built for survival. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:32.440
have incredibly low metabolic rates, meaning they&nbsp;
need to eat less food and can spend longer in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:36.760
air without stopping for a snack. Which comes&nbsp;
in handy, because it’s been known to travel up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:43.440
to 5,000 miles in one hunting trip, just to&nbsp;
find squid to feed to its young. That’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:49.000
equivalent of me flying from London to Los&nbsp;
Angeles just to grab some sushi for lunch.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:54.120
Yet, despite their fascinating adaptations&nbsp;
and incredible feats of endurance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:58.800
one of the most enchanting moments in&nbsp;
the life of a Wandering Albatross comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:03.000
when they do touch ground - which&nbsp;
they might only do once every few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:09.600
years. And what brings them back to&nbsp;
dry land, you ask? Sex, obviously.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:16.320
Suddenly, the islands dotting the Arctic&nbsp;
Ocean become the stage for a mesmerising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:22.680
dance. Both males and females engage in a series&nbsp;
of coordinated movements - they preen each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:27.360
then do a bit of fencing with their bills - then&nbsp;
they make a selection of really fucking weird&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:32.840
sounds in each other’s faces. Specifically,&nbsp;
they are known to perform sky calling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:37.200
where they point their bills at the sky&nbsp;
and make a chilling screaming sound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:45.560
which kinda sounds like a thousand&nbsp;
people burning in the fires of Mordor.

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:51.440
An albatross can sometimes court its potential&nbsp;
lover for hours. But it’s important to be picky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:57.120
because once a bond is established, Albatross&nbsp;
are monogamous, and they remain faithful to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:03.320
that partner for life. Which sounds impressive,&nbsp;
but then you remember it spends most of its life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:12.000
on a sea-bound solo stag do - doing summersaults,&nbsp;
thousands of miles from home, for years at a time.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:17.600
But even in their moments of solitude, when&nbsp;
their mate might be thousands of miles away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:21.880
some believe they stay connected&nbsp;
through the wind - people (mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:26.040
hippies) say an Albatross’&nbsp;
partner can hear its calls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:33.080
even on the other side of the planet. A poetic&nbsp;
notion, that is obviously complete bollocks.

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:39.600
And yet, for all their majesty and marvel, the&nbsp;
Wandering Albatross is vulnerable. Their primary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:44.760
threats being longline fishing and plastic&nbsp;
pollution. These birds often mistake plastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:52.640
for prey, ingesting it and feeding it to their&nbsp;
chicks. The consequences can be lethal. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:58.000
they frequently find themselves at the end of&nbsp;
longline fishing hooks, and drown. Watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:05.480
such a formidable beast perish in such a cruel&nbsp;
and unfair way is a very sad thing indeed.

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:09.680
The wonderful Wandering Albatross has&nbsp;
inspired countless stories, legends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:15.040
and songs over the centuries. They remind us&nbsp;
that, no matter how difficult life may seem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:20.160
and no matter how impossible the challenges&nbsp;
that lay in our paths - we can always take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:26.200
a moment and awe at the delicate dance of a&nbsp;
beautiful bird as it soars across the ocean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:34.960
proving that limits are made to be broken.

